<article style="max-width:70ch;">
<h1>Measuring your pupillary distance</h1>
<p class="muted">PD is the distance between the centres of your pupils, in millimetres.</p>

<h2>Why it matters</h2>
<p>Each lens has an optical centre — the point designed to sit directly in front
of your pupil. If the PD is wrong the centres sit off to one side, which causes
eye strain and headaches even when every other number is correct.</p>

<h2>Letting the try-on measure it</h2>
<p>Open the <a href="/try-on">virtual try-on</a> and upload a photo or use your
camera. The tool finds your irises and uses them as a ruler: the visible iris of
an adult eye is 11.7&nbsp;mm across and varies remarkably little between people,
so it gives a reliable scale in any photograph.</p>
<p>Expect the result to be accurate to about &plusmn;2&nbsp;mm. We show a
confidence score alongside it, and we will not report a figure at all if the
photo makes it unreliable — a head turned away from the camera, for instance.</p>

<h2>Getting a good measurement</h2>
<ul>
<li>Face the camera straight on, with your head level.</li>
<li>Use even lighting, with nothing covering your eyes.</li>
<li>Keep your whole face in the frame.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Measuring it yourself</h2>
<p>Stand about 20&nbsp;cm from a mirror with a ruler against your brow. Line the
zero mark up with the centre of your right pupil and read off the millimetre
mark at the centre of your left pupil. Most adults fall between 54 and 74&nbsp;mm.</p>

<div class="alert alert-info">
Whichever way you measure it, one of our opticians confirms the PD before your
lenses are cut.
</div>
</article>
